$DIR/main.p:22:3: cannot pass &v to put, which keeps it after this iteration: the loop at line 20 reuses v in every iteration
    &v is a pointer to v. v lives in main's frame, where every iteration of the
    loop at line 20 reuses the same memory for it. put stores it in buf[i]
    (main.p:10). When the next iteration starts, what put kept would see that
    iteration's v, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    If put needs the view only during the call, change it so it does not keep
    it. To use the value later, keep the value instead of a view of it:
        var saved int // declared before the loop
        ...
        saved = v
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:24:3: cannot pass f to putFn, which keeps it after this iteration: it uses i, which the loop at line 20 reuses in every iteration
    A function literal refers to the variables it uses where they live, and i
    lives in main's frame, where every iteration of the loop at line 20 reuses
    the same memory for it. putFn stores it in fs[i] (main.p:12). When the next
    iteration starts, the literal kept by putFn would see that iteration's i,
    not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    If putFn needs the function literal only during the call, change it so it
    does not keep it. To use i later, keep its value instead of the function
    literal:
        var saved int // declared before the loop
        ...
        saved = i
    Or have the function literal take i as a parameter, and pass it where the
    literal is called.
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:25:3: cannot pass &Point{...} to putPoint, which keeps it after this iteration: the loop at line 20 makes it in the same memory in every iteration
    A composite literal whose address is taken (at main.p:25) is made in main's
    frame, as a local variable would be.
    Every iteration of the loop at line 20 reuses that memory. putPoint stores
    it in ps[i] (main.p:14). When the next iteration starts, what putPoint kept
    would see that iteration's value, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    Change putPoint so it does not keep the view, or pass a copy of the value
    instead of a view of it.
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)
